{
  "id": "b2-erase-negative",
  "courseId": "cpp-book-02",
  "chapter": 15,
  "title": "Erase every negative element safely",
  "level": "Debug",
  "prompt": "Change a vector in place so it keeps only nonnegative values, in the same order. Zero stays. All iterators used by this function must be obtained and used before an invalidating erase.",
  "signature": "void eraseNegative(std::vector<int>& values)",
  "starter": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid eraseNegative(std::vector<int>& values){auto p=std::find_if(values.begin(),values.end(),[](int n){return n<0;});if(p!=values.end())values.erase(p);}\n",
  "solution": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nvoid eraseNegative(std::vector<int>& values){values.erase(std::remove_if(values.begin(),values.end(),[](int n){return n<0;}),values.end());}\n",
  "driver": "int main(){std::vector<int> v;for(int x;std::cin>>x;)v.push_back(x);eraseNegative(v);for(int x:v)std::cout<<x<<\" \";std::cout<<\"\\n\";}\n",
  "sampleInput": "-2 0 4 -3 5\n",
  "sampleOutput": "0 4 5 \n",
  "tests": [
    {
      "id": "case1",
      "label": "Several negatives",
      "expression": "([]{std::vector<int> v{-2,0,4,-3,5};eraseNegative(v);return v==std::vector<int>({0,4,5});})()",
      "expected": "true",
      "hint": "Remove all matches, not just the first."
    },
    {
      "id": "case2",
      "label": "All negatives",
      "expression": "([]{std::vector<int> v{-1,-2};eraseNegative(v);return v.empty();})()",
      "expected": "true",
      "hint": "The new logical end may be begin()."
    },
    {
      "id": "case3",
      "label": "None negative",
      "expression": "([]{std::vector<int> v{0,3};eraseNegative(v);return v==std::vector<int>({0,3});})()",
      "expected": "true",
      "hint": "Do not remove zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "case4",
      "label": "Empty",
      "expression": "([]{std::vector<int> v;eraseNegative(v);return v.empty();})()",
      "expected": "true",
      "hint": "The empty range is valid."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "remove_if rearranges retained elements and returns a new logical end.",
    "erase removes the trailing elements from that end to values.end()."
  ],
  "explanation": "Vector erase invalidates iterators at and after the removed position. This erase-remove operation uses its iterator before erase changes the vector; it does not keep a stale iterator.",
  "chapterTitle": "Traversing Containers with Iterators"
}
